@page "/api-docs"
@using Pizza.Contracts
@inject PriceDisplay Prices

<PageTitle>API documentation</PageTitle>

<section class="page-heading">
    <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">DEVELOPER REFERENCE</p>
        <h1>API documentation</h1>
        <p class="text-secondary">Catalog and delivery APIs provided by Pizza Kitchen.</p>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="management-panel">
    <h2>Before you start</h2>
    <p>Catalog and Orders are separate services. Send <code>/api/catalog/*</code> requests to the Catalog origin
        and <code>/api/orders/*</code> requests to the Orders origin. This reference is available at
        <code>/api-docs</code> on both services. JSON property names are camelCase; send
        <code>Content-Type: application/json</code> for JSON request bodies. IDs are UUIDs.</p>
    <p><strong>MVP only:</strong> these APIs have no authentication or authorization. Do not send real customer
        data or expose them publicly without access controls. Each service permits 120 API requests per minute
        per remote IP, with no queue; the current limiter returns HTTP 503 when exhausted.</p>
    <p>Prices are decimal amounts, not minor units. No API payload carries a currency code and no conversion is
        performed. Configure the same <code>Pizza:CurrencyCode</code> on both services.
        This service currently displays: <strong>@(Prices.CurrencyCode ?? "currency not configured")</strong>.
        Without configuration, the UI shows amounts without a currency symbol rather than guessing a currency.</p>
</section>

<section class="management-panel">
    <h2>Catalog endpoints</h2>
    <div class="table-responsive">
        <table class="table">
            <thead><tr><th>Method and route</th><th>Request</th><th>Success response</th><th>Errors</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>GET /api/catalog/pizzas</code></td><td>None</td><td>200: PizzaItem[] (empty array when no pizzas)</td><td></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>GET /api/catalog/pizzas/{id}</code></td><td>UUID path parameter</td><td>200: PizzaItem</td><td>404: not found</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>POST /api/catalog/pizzas</code></td><td>PizzaUpsertRequest</td><td>201: PizzaItem; Location header points to the new pizza</td><td>400: validation problem</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>PUT /api/catalog/pizzas/{id}</code></td><td>PizzaUpsertRequest (full replacement of editable fields)</td><td>200: PizzaItem (image is preserved)</td><td>400: validation problem; 404: not found</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>DELETE /api/catalog/pizzas/{id}</code></td><td>None</td><td>204: no body</td><td>404: not found</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>POST /api/catalog/pizzas/{id}/image</code></td><td>multipart/form-data; file field named <code>file</code></td><td>200: updated PizzaItem</td><td>400: invalid image; 404: pizza not found; 413: request too large</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>GET /api/catalog/images/{key}</code></td><td>Image key from an uploaded imageUrl</td><td>200: binary image with its image Content-Type</td><td>400: invalid key; 404: not found</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <h3>Request and response shapes</h3>
    <p><code>PizzaUpsertRequest</code>: name (required, nonblank, trimmed length 1-100), description
        (required, nonblank, trimmed length 1-500), price (number, greater than 0 and at most 10000),
        isAvailable (boolean, optional, defaults to true). The server trims name and description.</p>
    <pre><code>@("""
        {
          "name": "Margherita",
          "description": "Tomato sauce, mozzarella, fresh basil",
          "price": 10.50,
          "isAvailable": true
        }
        """)</code></pre>
    <p><code>PizzaItem</code> response example (IDs are illustrative; use the ID returned by your request):</p>
    <pre><code>@("""
        {
          "id": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001",
          "name": "Margherita",
          "description": "Tomato sauce, mozzarella, fresh basil",
          "price": 10.50,
          "imageUrl": "/images/pizza.svg",
          "isAvailable": true
        }
        """)</code></pre>
    <p>New pizzas use the default image. Upload photos after creation: JPEG, PNG or WebP only, from 1 byte
        through 5 MiB (5,242,880 bytes), with a matching file signature and MIME type. Total multipart
        request limit is 5 MiB + 64 KiB. Uploaded imageUrl values are relative to the Catalog origin;
        seeded/default images are served as static assets under <code>/images/</code>.</p>
    <h3>Catalog examples (curl)</h3>
    <p>Replace <code>CATALOG_ORIGIN</code>, <code>PIZZA_ID</code> and <code>IMAGE_KEY</code>. Save the
        request above as <code>pizza.json</code>. On Windows, use <code>curl.exe</code>.</p>
    <pre><code>@("""
        curl CATALOG_ORIGIN/api/catalog/pizzas
        curl CATALOG_ORIGIN/api/catalog/pizzas/PIZZA_ID
        curl -i -X POST CATALOG_ORIGIN/api/catalog/pizzas -H "Content-Type: application/json" --data-binary "@pizza.json"
        curl -X PUT CATALOG_ORIGIN/api/catalog/pizzas/PIZZA_ID -H "Content-Type: application/json" --data-binary "@pizza.json"
        curl -X POST CATALOG_ORIGIN/api/catalog/pizzas/PIZZA_ID/image -F "file=@pizza.png;type=image/png"
        curl CATALOG_ORIGIN/api/catalog/images/IMAGE_KEY --output photo.png
        curl -i -X DELETE CATALOG_ORIGIN/api/catalog/pizzas/PIZZA_ID
        """)</code></pre>
</section>

<section class="management-panel">
    <h2>Orders endpoints</h2>
    <div class="table-responsive">
        <table class="table">
            <thead><tr><th>Method and route</th><th>Request</th><th>Success response</th><th>Errors</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>GET /api/orders/</code></td><td>None</td><td>200: PizzaOrder[] (empty array when no orders)</td><td></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>GET /api/orders/{id}</code></td><td>UUID path parameter</td><td>200: PizzaOrder</td><td>404: not found</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>POST /api/orders/</code></td><td>CreateOrderRequest</td><td>201: PizzaOrder; Location header points to the new order</td><td>400: validation problem; 503: catalog unavailable</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>PUT /api/orders/{id}/status</code></td><td>UpdateOrderStatusRequest</td><td>200: updated PizzaOrder</td><td>400: validation problem; 404: not found</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <h3>Request and response shapes</h3>
    <p><code>CreateOrderRequest</code>: customerName (required, nonblank, trimmed length 1-120),
        customerAddress (required, nonblank, trimmed length 1-500), items (1-20 entries).
        Each entry has pizzaId (UUID of an available catalog pizza) and quantity (integer, 1-50).
        The server reads prices and names from the Catalog and saves an order-time snapshot;
        clients cannot set prices, totals, IDs, creation time or initial status.</p>
    <pre><code>@("""
        {
          "customerName": "Example Customer",
          "customerAddress": "1 Example Street",
          "items": [
            { "pizzaId": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001", "quantity": 2 }
          ]
        }
        """)</code></pre>
    <p><code>PizzaOrder</code> response example:</p>
    <pre><code>@("""
        {
          "id": "10000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001",
          "customer": { "name": "Example Customer", "address": "1 Example Street" },
          "items": [
            {
              "pizzaId": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000001",
              "pizzaName": "Margherita",
              "unitPrice": 10.50,
              "quantity": 2,
              "lineTotal": 21.00
            }
          ],
          "status": "New",
          "createdAt": "2026-11-06T12:00:00+00:00",
          "total": 21.00
        }
        """)</code></pre>
    <p><code>UpdateOrderStatusRequest</code>: <code>{"status":"Preparing"}</code>.
        Status values in responses are strings. Orders start at <code>New</code> and can only advance
        one step: <code>New → Preparing → OutForDelivery → Completed</code>. Skipping, repeating
        or reversing stages is rejected. Completed orders cannot advance.</p>
    <h3>Orders examples (curl)</h3>
    <p>Replace <code>ORDERS_ORIGIN</code> and <code>ORDER_ID</code>. Save the create request as
        <code>order.json</code> and the status request as <code>status.json</code>.
        Use a real available pizza ID from the Catalog rather than assuming seeded IDs exist.</p>
    <pre><code>@("""
        curl ORDERS_ORIGIN/api/orders/
        curl ORDERS_ORIGIN/api/orders/ORDER_ID
        curl -i -X POST ORDERS_ORIGIN/api/orders/ -H "Content-Type: application/json" --data-binary "@order.json"
        curl -X PUT ORDERS_ORIGIN/api/orders/ORDER_ID/status -H "Content-Type: application/json" --data-binary "@status.json"
        """)</code></pre>
</section>

<section class="management-panel">
    <h2>Errors and health</h2>
    <p>Service validation failures use HTTP 400 and a JSON validation problem. Catalog and order creation
        errors use <code>errors.request</code>; order status errors use <code>errors.status</code>.
        Malformed JSON or parameter binding can also return 400. Upload validation returns a JSON
        object with an <code>error</code> string. Missing resources or invalid routes return 404;
        status-code pages may return HTML rather than JSON, so check the response Content-Type.</p>
    <pre><code>@("""
        {
          "type": "https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc9110#section-15.5.1",
          "title": "One or more validation errors occurred.",
          "status": 400,
          "errors": {
            "request": ["Price must be greater than 0 and no more than 10,000."]
          }
        }
        """)</code></pre>
    <p>If Orders cannot reach the Catalog during creation, HTTP 503 returns a problem with
        title <code>Catalog unavailable</code> and detail
        <code>The order was not created. Please retry when the catalog is available.</code></p>
    <p>Both services provide <code>GET /health/ready</code>: HTTP 200, text <code>Healthy</code>
        when healthy (503 when unhealthy). No external dependency probes are registered, so this
        checks the process, not Cosmos DB, Storage, SQL or Catalog connectivity.</p>
    <pre><code>curl CATALOG_ORIGIN/health/ready
curl ORDERS_ORIGIN/health/ready</code></pre>
</section>
